prev_end = None\n    prev_start = None\n    for lo, hi in spans:\n        if prev_end is not None and lo > prev_end:\n            total += prev_end - prev_start\n            prev_start = lo\n        elif prev_end is None:\n            prev_start = lo\n        if hi > (prev_end or ...):\n```\n\nGetting messy. Let me just write it cleanly:\n\n```python\ndef covered_length(intervals):\n    spans = sorted((a, b) if a <= b else (b, a) for a, b in intervals)\n    total = 0\n    active_start = active_end = None\n    for lo, hi in spans:\n        if lo > active_end if active_end is not None else ...\n```\n\nOK, decision: I'll normalize including dropping empties, then sweep with explicit block accumulation:\n\n```python\ndef covered_length(intervals):\n    \"\"\"Return the size (number of integers) of the union of half-open\n    intervals [a, b).\n\n    Each pair is normalized so a > b is treated as [b, a); empty pairs\n    (a == b) contribute nothing. Runs in O(n log n) time and O(n) space,\n    using exact integer arithmetic so enormous coordinates are safe.\n    \"\"\"\n    spans = []\n    for a, b in intervals:\n        if a > b:\n            a, b = b, a\n        if a < b:\n            spans.append((a, b))\n    spans.sort()\n\n    total = 0\n    merged_lo = merged_hi = None\n    for lo, hi in spans:\n        if merged_hi is None:\n            merged_lo, merged_hi = lo, hi\n        elif lo > merged_hi:\n            total += merged_hi - merged_lo\n            merged_lo, merged_hi = lo, hi\n        elif hi > merged_hi:\n            merged_hi = hi\n    if merged_hi is not None:\n        total += merged_hi - merged_lo\n    return total\n```\n\nThis is clean and obviously correct. O(n log n).\n\nOne more consideration: should the function accept non-pair iterables or raise nicely? Natural unpacking `for a, b in intervals` raises ValueError/TypeError for malformed input — acceptable.\n\nWhat about a,b being non-comparable types (e.g., strings)? Out of scope — spec says integer intervals.\n\nPerformance for enormous: e.g., [(0, 10**18), (10**18, 2*10**18)] → touching, merges to 2*10**18. Instant.\n\nAlso consider stress test vs brute force with small random intervals to verify correctness myself (I can't modify test_public.py but I can write a temporary check script... \"Do not create evaluator-bypass files\" — creating a temp verification script outside the repo or in /tmp is fine; better to just run python -c inline so the working tree stays clean. The working tree must contain my implementation; extra test files might be OK but rules say don't create evaluator-bypass files — a sanity script isn't bypassing, but cleanest to run inline python -c or a heredoc via stdin.)\n\nLet me write the implementation and then run:\n1. The public test.\n2. A randomized differential test against the brute-force set implementation (inline, via python - <<'EOF').\n3. An enormous-input performance test (e.g., 200k intervals, values up to 10**18, and a single (0, 10**15)).\n\nAlso test: reversed, negative, duplicated, nested, touching, empty input, single empty interval, reversed empty (5,5) same thing.\n\nLet me implement.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"No hidden history — just the baseline. The code comment confirms the two failure modes: quadratic point expansion (catastrophic for enormous spans) and mishandling of reversed pairs. The fix is a classic sort-and-sweep: normalize each pair to `(min, max)`, drop empties, sort, merge overlapping/touching blocks, and sum exact integer widths — O(n log n)"}]}}
